WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.209 --> 00:00:06.180
South Park is still just as vital and relevant
as was when it premiered in 1997.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.080
Creators Trey Parker and Matt Stone have since
become a comedy brand unto themselves with

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.610
movies like 2004's Team America: World Police
and the hit Broadway musical The Book of Mormon.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.220
Let's take a look at the little cartoon that
started it all and discover the untold truth

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.840
of South Park.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.179
Viral VHS

00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:25.221
When Parker and Stone were film students in
1992, they used construction paper and an

00:00:25.221 --> 00:00:29.679
old 8 millimeter camera to create a crude,
animated short called The Spirit of Christmas.

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:33.359
Featuring a group of foul-mouthed Colorado
kids, it was a massive hit at campus screenings.

00:00:33.359 --> 00:00:39.780
“Dude, I told you not to put the F***ing
hat on frosty’s F***ing head.”

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.550
Parker and Stone didn't think about it much
until they moved to Los Angeles in 1993 and

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.550
their indie movie Cannibal!

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.859
The Musical caught the attention of FOX executive
Brian Graden.

00:00:47.859 --> 00:00:52.670
He loved The Spirit of Christmas so much that
he made 100 VHS copies and sent them as holiday

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:53.670
cards.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.500
Two years later, Graden gave the pair $2,000
to make another, and they delivered Jesus

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.100
vs. Santa, which he sent to 40 people and
was widely bootlegged around town.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.370
“I began to think it was going to take off
when I went to parties and people kept trying

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:08.380
to put it in and show me my own christmas
card unaware that it was my christmas card.”

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:12.490
George Clooney reportedly distributed 300
copies to his friends, and within a year,

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.950
multiple TV networks asked Parker and Stone
to pitch a show.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.070
Based On Real People

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.360
Parker and Stone drew inspiration from their
own lives to create many of the citizens of

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:21.670
South Park.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:26.030
Cartman's promiscuous mom Liane is named after
Parker's ex-fiancée; Butters is based on

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.880
the duo's childhood friend and South Park
animation director Eric Stough; and Cartman

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.400
is named after their high school friend Matt
Karpman.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.420
Even Mr. Hankey, the Christmas Poo, comes
from Parker's childhood.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:38.530
When he'd forget to flush the toilet, his
dad would warn him that Mr. Hankey would crawl

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.399
out of the toilet and eat him.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:41.399
Fast Turnaround

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:45.240
Most animated series can take up to a year
to produce a single episode, but an average

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.750
South Park episode has a production schedule
of just six days.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:52.539
That means the show can comment on breaking
news almost as fast as live-action shows,

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:54.570
but the process can be grueling.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.950
Parker and Stone lead a team that writes,
records, and animates using a program called

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:02.020
Maya, often turning in completed episodes
the same day they're scheduled to air.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.820
And unlike many other long-running shows,
South Park's creators remain extremely involved

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.390
in the day-to-day operations.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:12.790
They've only missed a deadline once: in 2013,
when their studios lost power.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:13.960
Teamwork

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.910
Most episodes credit only Trey Parker and
Matt Stone as writers, but South Park employs

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.250
a large staff of writers and consultants.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:27.050
Lots of famous names have popped into the
writers room, including SNL’s Bill Hader.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.541
Kristen Schaal wrote for a dozen episodes
in 2007; and sitcom legend Norman Lear, creator

00:02:31.541 --> 00:02:35.250
of All in the Family and Good Times, even
dropped by at age 81.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.450
Lear served as a creative consultant on the
episode where the boys learn Earth is just

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:43.180
the set of a reality show created by aliens,
so the man who created Archie Bunker also

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.560
brought us the ice cream-pooping taco.

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:50.930
Fishsticks

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:54.170
One of the things South Park does best is
tear down celebrities.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:58.290
While most celebrities the show has skewered
have taken it in stride, Kanye West, unsurprisingly,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.290
did not.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:00.290
“Alright, that does it.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.930
I am going to kick your mother f***ing ass.”

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.920
In an infamous episode, Kanye doesn't understand
a joke about fish sticks.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.010
Real-life Kanye was also pretty upset, and
dissed South Park in his 2010 song "Gorgeous,"

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.620
citing his intention to, well...

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.920
“Choke a south park writer with a fishstick.”

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.300
...oooookay then.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.310
Poor Kenny

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.700
“Oh my god, they killed Kenny.”

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.080
“You bastards.”

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.220
South Park's concept of killing off Kenny
in every single episode, only to have him

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:36.070
return for the next episode, is funny and
innovative, but not entirely original.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:40.930
Parker and Stone got the idea from MTV's '90s
sci-fi cartoon Aeon Flux, in which the main

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.930
character frequently died.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.370
Parker and Stone also drew inspiration from
one of their childhood friends, also named

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.230
Kenny, who skipped school so often that rumors
spread that he'd died.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.500
“My friend Kenny who at the bus stop he
had the little orange coat.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.340
He would always say **** we couldn’t understand.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:58.200
And were like, we can’t ****ing understand
you, ya know.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.480
And ah but he, and he was the poorest kid
in the neighborhood and that’s why ya know

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:05.630
it was always like, that’s why we were always
like he would always like disap, what happened

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:06.840
to Kenny, is he dead?

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.340
The cartoon Kenny doesn't always die in later
seasons, although he was semi-permanently

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:15.380
killed off for a while in season 5 until Parker
decided to bring him back.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:16.380
Timmy!

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.900
Confined to a wheelchair and afflicted with
an unspecified condition that only allows

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:24.580
him to say his own name, Comedy Central reportedly
didn't want Timmy on South Park.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:25.590
“Timmy”

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.561
Executives were worried Parker and Stone would
make fun of people with disabilities, but

00:04:28.561 --> 00:04:31.569
the duo countered that the other kids would
treat him as just one of the gang.

00:04:31.569 --> 00:04:34.970
Fans immediately took to the character, and
Parker claimed people began shouting "Timmy!"

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:36.910
at him on the street instead of the usual...

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.080
“Oh my god, they killed Kenny.”

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.080
“You bastards.”

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.500
Many critics praised South Park for including
Timmy, and later another physically disabled

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.120
character, Jimmy, for raising awareness of
issues facing the handicapped.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.419
Disabled viewers voted Timmy the "Greatest
Disabled TV Character" in the 2008 RADAR People

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:52.419
of the Year Awards.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:57.740
“Timmy, Ti, Ti, TImmy.”

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.439
Thanks for watching!

00:04:59.439 --> 00:05:02.240
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.539
And leave us a comment to let us know which
episode of South Park is your favorite…

